= Application-specific integrated circuit: simple explanation =

<onlyinclude>An '''application-specific integrated circuit (ASIC)''' is a chip designed for a particular set of jobs. Unlike a field-programmable gate array (FPGA), its main hardware connections are established during manufacture rather than rewritten by a user after purchase.</onlyinclude>

== Why build one? ==
A custom chip can avoid some overhead of general-purpose or reconfigurable hardware. It may use less energy, run faster, or occupy less silicon for a particular workload. Those benefits depend on the design and production technology; an ASIC is not automatically superior.

== What does it take? ==
Engineers describe and test the design, convert it to physical circuit layouts, check timing and manufacturing rules, then arrange fabrication and test the finished chips. This requires substantial upfront work. A mistake found after manufacturing can be expensive to correct.

== When does it make sense? ==
An ASIC can make economic sense when a stable design is produced in enough units to recover engineering and tooling costs. An FPGA is often attractive for prototypes, lower volumes or requirements that may change.

